.env
.env_vllm
*__pycache__
.pytest_cache
scicom

# generated test/inference audio (keep the folder via audio/.gitkeep)
audio/*.wav
audio/*.pcm
audio
*.sbatch
# fastText language-id model downloaded by bench/langid_compare.py
bench/results/lid.176.ftz

# multilingual normalizer dataset: regenerable in seconds/minutes from the cached LLM files (kept)
bench/results/multilingual_normalizer/template_pairs.jsonl
bench/results/multilingual_normalizer/train*.jsonl
bench/results/multilingual_normalizer/val*.jsonl
bench/results/multilingual_normalizer/test*.jsonl
# synthetic-normalizer/ is the standalone copy of that pipeline with its own data/ —
# same rule, same reason. The splits are published as
# huggingface.co/datasets/Scicom-intl/Multilingual-Normalizer, so the HF dataset is the
# source of truth and these are a 105 MB duplicate of it; `train_sft.jsonl` alone is
# 60 MB and trips GitHub's large-file warning on every push. The cached LLM files
# (llm_*.jsonl, templates_llm.jsonl) stay tracked: they cost API calls to recreate,
# and `build` regenerates everything below from them in minutes.
synthetic-normalizer/data/template_pairs.jsonl
synthetic-normalizer/data/train*.jsonl
synthetic-normalizer/data/val*.jsonl
synthetic-normalizer/data/test*.jsonl

# Bench audio is regenerable bulk and the reports carry the numbers — track the JSON,
# never the wavs. bench/results/pitch_stress alone is 60 MB of them.
bench/results/**/*.wav
bench/results/pitch_stress/
